    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d612">

                <pb facs="1130" n="1114" xml:id="pg1114"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">261.1122/2–2850: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Barbour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-02-28T23:00:00+03:00">February 28,
                            1950—11 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>705. Called Foreign Office 10 p. m., by Gromyko who handed me <hi
                        rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> translation of which contained
                    immediately following telegram<anchor n="1" xml:id="d612-f1"/> (Deptel 92,
                    February 1<anchor n="2" xml:id="d612-f2"/>). Beyond stating <hi
                        rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> is reply to our <hi
                        rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> December 12, 1949, Gromyko made no
                    comment and meeting lasted less than minute.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Barbour</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d612-f1" n="1" xml:id="d612-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. The text of the <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> of
                    February 28 regarding the continued refusal to grant exit permits to American
                    citizens and spouses of American citizens to leave the Soviet Union is printed
                    in Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, March 20, 1950,
                    pp. 440–441. It was in response to a letter dated October 4, 1949, from
                    Ambassador Kirk to the Acting Minister of Foreign Affairs of the Soviet Union
                    Andrey Andreyevich Gromyko, as well as to the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> of December 12, 1949. The text of both documents is in
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, pp. 434–440. See also <ref
                        target="frus1949v05#pg_684">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1949, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, p. 684</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d612-f2" n="2" xml:id="d612-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed. Ambassador of the Soviet Union Aleksander Semenovich Panyushkin had
                    been handed a copy of the United States <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> of December 12, 1949, with a request to expedite a reply,
                    on January 18, 1950, as printed in the Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, March 20, 1950, p. 433. However, in
                    telegram 92 to the Embassy in the Soviet Union on February 1, it was stated that
                    the Ambassador had been handed the copy by Secretary of State Acheson on that
                    afternoon. (261.1122/2–150)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
